From 81e55ffcd0ef45ebb505fd890873551fa928afe0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 01:39:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] DeepSeek V4.1 MoE: the routed SwiGLU and MXFP8 cast over the
 live tiles

TileKernels' swiglu_forward_and_per_token_cast takes about 22 us a call at
every decode size: each of its 1,024-thread CTAs walks all 384 experts'
boundaries, one at a time, before it reaches a row. moe_swiglu_cast reads the
dispatch layout instead: a CTA takes 4 rows of one 32-row tile (2 when a row
has fewer than four warps, TP3's 768 columns), issues all of their loads
before any arithmetic, and the CTAs of tiles past num_tiles stop at once, so
a step pays for its live rows only.

The arithmetic is TileKernels', element for element: route weights, the clamp
by compare and select (spark-ds41f's TileKernels patch 0001: a NaN passes, as
through torch.clamp), the fast __expf/__fdividef product with its certificate
and precise fallback, the same vector width and get_sf_and_inv. A warp takes
the same 32 * vec consecutive columns of one row as a TileKernels 32-thread
group, so the certificate's warp vote covers the same elements and every byte
and scale equals TileKernels'.

On GB10 (spark-ds41f experiments/2026-10-18-r6w-swiglu): 1.5-12.8 us against
21.8-23.5 us at 1-96 rows (TP4, warm), and no size slower, warm or cold, at
TP4, TP3 or the drafter's shapes, up to 8,192 rows.

Test: every live row's E4M3 bytes and UE8M0 scales against TileKernels' kernel
on real dispatch layouts (576, 768 and 256 columns; 1 to 700 tokens), with
gates past the fast exp's domain and the smallest scales, and a NaN gate value
reaching its row.
